NAME
    pager - the --More-- pager of more, man and md
            (#include "../lib_more.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_more.c"      (includes lib_fs.c)
    while (!pgquit && (c = fgetc(h)) != 65535) pgc(c);

DESCRIPTION
    pgc(c)   prints c; after every PGLINES (23) line feeds it shows
             "--More--", waits for a key from keyin() (the keyboard,
             no echo, never redirected) and wipes the prompt line
             (CR, spaces, CR):
                 space, other   the next page
                 Enter          one more line (CR or LF)
                 q, Q, Ctrl-D   quit: pgquit becomes 1 and pgc prints
                                nothing more
    pgs(s)   pgc() over a string
    pgquit   1 once the reader quit: stop producing output
    pgrows   the lines shown since the last prompt

    A screen is 24 lines: 23 of text and the prompt line. Everything a
    command prints must go through pgc(), or the count goes wrong.
    When stdout is a > file or a pipe (stdio() bit 1, asked at the
    first line feed) pgc() only prints: nothing is paged into a file.

NOTES
    On the instruction-level emulator a lower-case q is read as end of
    input (keyin() returns 65535), which quits as well.
    The key came from conin() until 2026-09-23; since the shell has
    pipes, conin() is stdin and the key must be keyin().
    The page is named pager because `more` is the command's page.
    Ported from the prompt() of P8X os/commands/more.c 2026-09-23 (the
    P8X md had its own copy at 22 lines); Enter is CR or LF here,
    since the emulators deliver a session's line ends as LF.

SEE ALSO
